    "training_evaluation": "CHANGES: Category: Training Strategy & Ensembling. What: Replace single 80/20 split with 5-Fold Stratified Cross-Validation to generate out-of-fold (OOF) probability predictions and average test predictions across folds. Why: Single 80/20 split underuses 20% of training data and suffers from split variance, whereas 5-Fold OOF predictions enable leak-free optimization of ensemble weights and class thresholds across the full training dataset. How: Train LightGBM, XGBoost, CatBoost, and TabularResNet on each fold, store OOF probability predictions, optimize ensemble weights and class decision thresholds via Nelder-Mead on full OOF predictions, and average test predictions across all 5 folds. Interface constraints: Maintain target encoding mapping {'at-risk': 0, 'fit': 1, 'unhealthy': 2}, output submission to `./submission/submission.csv`, and print `Final Validation Score: {score}` computed using balanced accuracy."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current baseline achieves strong performance with an ensemble, but XGBoost is trained without class balance weighting, limiting its alignment with the evaluation metric. Additionally, feature engineering lacks critical domain-specific non-linear physiological metrics (such as sleep deficit and heart rate anomaly deviations), and the ensemble blending relies on fixed heuristic weights. Enhancing domain feature extraction and optimizing both XGBoost training sample weights and dynamic model blend parameters will yield significant performance gains on balanced accuracy.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "CHANGES: Add non-linear clinical deviation features and group aggregations in data_processing_and_feature_engineering. WHAT: Compute ideal sleep deficit `abs(sleep_duration - 7.5)`, resting heart rate anomaly `abs(heart_rate - 70.0)`, BMR metabolic proxy interactions, and missing interaction terms, alongside multi-group aggregations computed strictly on train data. WHY: Health classification relies heavily on non-linear physiological departures from healthy normal ranges, which simple linear ratios fail to capture. HOW: Modify `engineer_features()` to create deviation metrics and add new group stats aggregated on `['smoking_alcohol', 'stress_level']` fitted strictly on `df_train`. Interface constraints: Preserve `X_train_feat`, `X_val_feat`, `X_test_feat`, and column data types.", "training_evaluation": "CHANGES: Apply balanced sample weighting to XGBoost and optimize model blend weights dynamically in training_evaluation. WHAT: Compute balanced class sample weights for XGBoost fitting and upgrade ensemble post-processing to optimize model weights jointly with class thresholds. WHY: XGBoost was previously unweighted, giving uncalibrated probabilities on imbalanced classes, while fixed blending weights (0.35, 0.30, 0.25, 0.10) sub-optimally combine model outputs. HOW: Pass `sample_weight=compute_sample_weight('balanced', y_train)` to `xgb_model.fit()`, and use `scipy.optimize.minimize` with Nelder-Mead on validation balanced accuracy to solve for optimal model blend coefficients and class multipliers simultaneously. Interface constraints: Maintain final output path `./submission/submission.csv` and keep `Final Validation Score` print statement."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current baseline achieves strong performance with an ensemble, but XGBoost is trained without class balance weighting, limiting its alignment with the evaluation metric. Additionally, feature engineering lacks critical domain-specific non-linear physiological metrics (such as sleep deficit and heart rate anomaly deviations), and the ensemble blending relies on fixed heuristic weights. Enhancing domain feature extraction and optimizing both XGBoost training sample weights and dynamic model blend parameters will yield significant performance gains on balanced accuracy.",
  "module": [
